#!/bin/bash
# A second pair of eyes on what a change looks like (Ben, 2026-11-18:
# "provide all screenshots of changes to a … agent … for a second review
# / set of eyes to identify anything you missed"; 2026-11-23, after a
# day of Gemini 4 Neon's looks: "for the visual reviews please go back
# to using astra 6.2 on high -- neon is missing too much stuff"): Astra
# 6.2 at high, in the Codex CLI, is shown the pictures and says what a
# careful designer would catch that the layout lint and `tsession
# review` can't: a line set right under another, uneven gaps, edges and
# baselines not lined up, text cut or crowded, something out of keeping
# with the rest of the window.
#
#   tools/dev/astra-look [--about "WHAT CHANGED"] PICTURE.png…
#
# PICTUREs: the change's pictures (headless: tools/dev/tsession shot),
# each state and scale worth seeing, in a folder of yours under /var/tmp.
# It prints its findings and writes them to astra-look.md beside the
# first picture (what it did, to astra-look.log). Check each finding
# against the picture yourself, and fix what's real. Beside
# tools/dev/opus-look (Opus 5.5's review of the design itself): every
# change's pictures go to both before it's committed.
#
# The look is a Codex in a tmux session, as every helper here is
# (`tools/dev/agents look`: this is that), so it can be read while it
# looks (`tools/dev/agents peek astra-FOLDER`) and told more (`agents
# say`). Run this in the background: it prints a line a minute of what
# the look is doing, and returns when the review is written (0), or as
# soon as the look needs you (3). It works on copies of the pictures in
# a folder of its own, may write there and nowhere else, has no network,
# and asks no leave (what its sandbox refuses is refused).
exec /usr/bin/python3 "$(dirname "$0")/agents" look --by astra "$@"
